    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "This chapter is for all professionals working together in the care of a young person: psychiatrists, mental health teams, nurses, specialists."
    },
    {
     "t": "h",
     "text": "How your role shifts in the forest"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "In a clinical room, you control the environment. In the forest, you do not. The session follows what the forest presents and what the young person does with it, not a pre-planned clinical agenda. You enter the forest with them, alongside them. It becomes a shared experience where the patient–practitioner boundaries can soften — in a good way."
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "If you are working alongside a forest professional, agree roles before you start. Who holds clinical safety? Who monitors the young person's mental state? Who leads on the trail? Your ability to step back and let the forest guide lead is as important as knowing when to step in."
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "You may not always be working alongside a separate forest guide. You may have trained to lead forest groups yourself, or have enough experience to work independently."
    },
    {
     "t": "h",
     "text": "Here's what good forest practice looks like"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Be led by openness. Effective forest work is about being present rather than doing or directing. Trust the process. Resist the urge to fill silences or redirect to planned exercises. Avoid positioning yourself as the expert. Be a reassuring, boundaried presence."
    },
    {
     "t": "h",
     "text": "Best practice for recording in the field"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Skip the digital tools. Use printed session record sheets or laminated prompt cards instead."
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Run a short structured debrief straight after each session: first with the young people, then separately with the adults. This closes the session and gives everyone space to process what happened."
    },
    {
     "t": "h",
     "text": "Find the right supervision"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Outdoor, uncontrolled environments will surface material that standard clinical supervision is not always designed to hold. Don't let this stop you bringing it to supervision."
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Where specialist supervision is not available, peer group supervision with colleagues doing similar work is a practical alternative. Professional networks and nature-based therapy associations in your area may help you find relevant expertise. This setting is different enough to warrant finding a space where that difference can be properly reflected on."
    },
    {
     "t": "quote",
     "text": "They all turned out differently than I had planned. That is actually quite nice. For me, that is a symbol of good therapy in the forest.",
     "by": "Mental health professional, Germany"
    }
